WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:05.180
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:10.559
Imagine the following: we put an object on
a robot arm, and the input is a recorded video

00:00:10.559 --> 00:00:11.559
of it.

00:00:11.559 --> 00:00:16.420
And the output would be the digital geometry
and a material model for this object.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:22.539
This geometry and material model we can plug
into a photorealistic light simulation program

00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:25.500
to have a digital copy of our real-world object.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:31.439
First, we have to be wary of the fact that
normally, solving such a problem sounds completely

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:32.439
hopeless.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:37.390
We have three variables that we have to take
into consideration: the lighting in the room,

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:41.520
the geometry of the object, and the material
properties of the object.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.610
If any two of the three variables is known,
the problem is relatively simple and there

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:50.110
are already existing works to address these
combinations.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:54.980
For instance, if we create a studio lighting
setup and know the geometry of the object

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:58.899
as well, it is not that difficult to capture
the material properties.

00:00:58.899 --> 00:01:04.440
Also, if the material properties and lighting
is known, there are methods to extract the

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.940
geometry of the objects.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:12.290
However, this is a way more difficult formulation
of the problem, because out of the three variables,

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:17.340
not two, not one, but zero are known.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.830
We don't have control over the lighting, the
material properties can be arbitrary, and

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.600
the geometry can also be anything.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.730
Several very sensible assumptions are being
made, such as that our camera has to be stationary

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:35.950
and the rotation directions of the object
should be known, and some more of these are

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.050
discussed in the paper in detail.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.680
All of them are quite sensible and they really
don't feel limiting.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.800
The algorithm works the following way: in
the first step, we estimate the lighting and

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.120
leaning on this estimation, we build a rough
initial surface model.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.540
And in the second step, using this surface
model, we see a bit clearer, and therefore

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:01.170
we can refine our initial guess for the lighting
and material model.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:06.710
However, now that we know the lighting a bit
better, we can again, get back to the surface

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:09.849
reconstruction and improve our solution there.

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:15.750
This entire process happens iteratively, which
means, that first, we obtain a very rough

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:20.950
initial guess for the surface, and we constantly
refine this piece of surface to get closer

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:28.560
and closer to the final solution.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:34.109
And now, feast your eyes on these incredible
results, and marvel at the fact that we know

00:02:34.109 --> 00:02:40.629
next to nothing about the input, and the geometry
and material properties almost magically appear

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:41.939
on the screen.

00:02:41.939 --> 00:02:46.920
This is an amazing piece of work, and lots
and lots of details and results are discussed

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.609
the paper, which is quite well written and
was a joy to read.

00:02:50.609 --> 00:02:54.360
Make sure to have a look at it, the link is
available in the video description.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:03:00.010
Also, thanks for all the kind comments, I've
experienced a recent influx of e-mails from

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:05.329
people all around the world expressing how
they are enjoying the series and many of them

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:10.819
telling their personal stories and their relation
to science, and how these new inventions are

00:03:10.819 --> 00:03:14.260
being talked about over dinner with the family
and relatives.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.200
It has been such a delight to read these messages.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:18.200
Thank you!

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.580
And also, thanks for watching and for your
generous support, and I'll see you next time!

